WEBVTT

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:02.000
Okay, let's rehearse your lines.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.200
Yeah, we're ready.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:04.240
Oh yeah, yeah, we're ready.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:05.813
Alice, you're frightened.

00:00:05.813 --> 00:00:08.960
You say: this isn't what I was expecting at all.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:12.815
Got it.

00:00:12.815 --> 00:00:15.840
This isn't what I was expecting at all.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.693
To date, creating video with AI

00:00:21.693 --> 00:00:24.160
hasn't resembled the way we direct film.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.239
What if it could?

00:00:26.239 --> 00:00:30.113
Pika Enact is an experimental platform

00:00:30.113 --> 00:00:33.231
that mimics being on set,

00:00:33.231 --> 00:00:36.160
where creation becomes immediate. Responsive.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:39.182
You give him a small nod and deliver your line.

00:00:39.182 --> 00:00:41.035
Like a secret? Exactly.

00:00:41.035 --> 00:00:42.400
Visceral.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.433
We adapted Pika H3 Flex

00:00:46.433 --> 00:00:49.000
to generate in a continuous stream,

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.429
fast enough to let creators shape a scene

00:00:51.429 --> 00:00:52.400
while it plays.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.400
Bob, now you step away.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.680
This opened up three new kinds of control.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.260
Live staging lets a creator drag actors and objects

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.240
to where they want them in the scene.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.280
Live direction allows for direct interaction with actors.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.337
Alice, this is where you snap.

00:01:21.337 --> 00:01:23.566
So this is where it really gets to you.

00:01:23.566 --> 00:01:25.280
A lot more emotional here.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.760
There is no us if I'm always alone.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:28.989
And live camera control

00:01:28.989 --> 00:01:31.600
to reframe the shot while it's playing.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.207
Let's do it for real now.

00:01:37.207 --> 00:01:38.880
First, bring the lights down.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.093
Oh, no, no. Too dark.

00:01:42.093 --> 00:01:44.240
Bring it back up and put them in a kitchen.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.791
Oh, that's perfect.

00:01:46.791 --> 00:01:50.800
Okay, let's run it from the top. And action.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.784
You said seven. It's almost midnight.

00:01:54.784 --> 00:01:55.600
I called you twice.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:58.328
And you could have called back,

00:01:58.328 --> 00:01:59.120
but you chose not to.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.205
I was working, Alice. I haven't stopped all day.

00:02:03.205 --> 00:02:05.600
I'm doing all of this for us. For both of us.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.066
There is no us if I'm always alone.

00:02:09.066 --> 00:02:10.160
And cut.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.285
To us, Enact represents the future of video creation,

00:02:14.285 --> 00:02:17.440
where there's no prompt box between you

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:18.899
and your actors,

00:02:18.899 --> 00:02:21.120
and directing feels surprisingly familiar.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.622
Thanks. That felt really good.

00:02:22.622 --> 00:02:23.200
Yeah, we got it.
